{#
  Project Image Caption Template
  
  Used for image captions everywhere: project pages, homepage, collection pages.

  The TITLE row shows `imageTitle` when the caller supplies one, and falls back to
  `projectTitle`. That distinction is the whole point of the parameter:

    - On a PROJECT PAGE every image is its own photograph and carries its own DAM title
      ("Wandskulptur Hinwil, full view" / "... test hanging"). The old site prints exactly
      that, per image, in the caption's <h3>.
    - On a CARD (homepage, collection, featured) one image stands for the whole project,
      so the PROJECT title is what identifies it. Those callers pass no imageTitle.

  Until 2026-09-09 every caller passed only projectTitle, so a project page repeated one
  title under all its photographs -- "Die grosse Hafenszene" seven times where the live
  site names each view. Because most DAM titles are "<project>, <view>", the result read
  as a caption TRUNCATED AT THE COMMA: 109 of 727 differ from the project title by exactly
  a punctuation mark plus a suffix. It was reported as truncated captions, and the effect
  is the same, but nothing was ever truncated -- the migration had every title in the front
  matter (796 of 796 images) and the template never asked for it.

  Required params:
  - projectTitle: The title of the project (string) -- the fallback for the title row
  - projectYear: The year of the project (string or number)

  Optional params:
  - imageTitle: The title of THIS image (string). Wins over projectTitle when present.
  - imageDescription: Description of the specific image (string)
  - imageAuthor: Author/photographer of the image (string)
  - projectSlug: For error reporting (string)
  - imageIndex: For error reporting (number)
  - context: Where this is being used, for error reporting (string, e.g. "featured-projects", "collection")
#}

{# The title actually rendered: the image's own when given, else the project's. #}
{% set captionTitle = imageTitle or projectTitle %}

{# Validate required parameters and log errors if missing (console only, not displayed) #}
{% if not captionTitle %}
  {% set errorMsg = "ERROR: Missing project title for image caption" %}
  {% if projectSlug %}{% set errorMsg = errorMsg + " in project '" + projectSlug + "'" %}{% endif %}
  {% if context %}{% set errorMsg = errorMsg + " (" + context + ")" %}{% endif %}
  {% set _ = errorMsg | log %}
{% endif %}

{% if not projectYear %}
  {% set errorMsg = "ERROR: Missing project year for image caption" %}
  {% if projectSlug %}{% set errorMsg = errorMsg + " in project '" + projectSlug + "'" %}{% endif %}
  {% if context %}{% set errorMsg = errorMsg + " (" + context + ")" %}{% endif %}
  {% set _ = errorMsg | log %}
{% endif %}

{# Render caption - only show lines with available data #}
<div class="project-image-caption">
  {#
    Top line: Title (left) and Year (right).

    The `and not imageDescription` guard that used to be here suppressed this row whenever
    an image had a description -- i.e. on essentially every real image. Figma shows all
    three rows together, always (node 274:3273, Project Card > Frame 2147238709: row 1
    Title+Year, row 2 Description, row 3 Author). Removed 2026-08-27; see PLAN.md
    "Project Page -- extracted spec".
  #}
  {% if captionTitle or projectYear %}
  <div class="caption-title-year">
    {% if captionTitle %}<span class="caption-title">{{ captionTitle }}</span>{% endif %}
    {% if projectYear %}<span class="caption-year">{{ projectYear }}</span>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}
  
  {# Description line (left-aligned) #}
  {% if imageDescription %}
  <div class="caption-description">{{ imageDescription }}</div>
  {% endif %}
  
  {# Bottom line: Author (left-aligned) #}
  {% if imageAuthor %}
  <div class="caption-author">{{ imageAuthor }}</div>
  {% endif %}
</div>
